luding acupuncture, physical activity, dietary modifications, mindfulness-based practices, and biofeedback. These methods are especially valuable for patients with poor medication adherence or those who experience adverse effects from pharmacological agents. Their integration into clinical care reflects a shift toward holistic, patient-centered migraine management. The high degree of relationships between 'non-drug treatment' and its subcomponents indicates its central role in the community [Data: Entities (239), Relationships (376, 377, 378, 381, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Non-drug treatments as a comprehensive migraine management approach\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Acupuncture is a well-established non-pharmacological intervention for migraine, frequently cited as an effective method in both acute and preventive contexts. It is included within the broader category of non-drug treatments and is supported by clinical evidence for reducing migraine frequency and severity. Its inclusion in the community reflects its acceptance in integrative medicine and its use as an alternative or adjunct to medication, particularly for patients seeking non-invasive options [Data: Entities (240), Relationships (376, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Acupuncture as a validated non-drug migraine therapy\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Physical activity is recognized as a key component of non-drug migraine management, contributing to both prevention and symptom reduction. Regular exercise has been associated with decreased migraine frequency and improved overall well-being in patients. It is particularly beneficial for individuals with lifestyle-related triggers and is often recommended as part of a comprehensive, non-pharmacological strategy. Its inclusion in the community highlights the importance of behavioral and lifestyle interventions in migraine care [Data: Entities (241), Relationships (377, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Exercise as a preventive and therapeutic tool in migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Dietary regulation is a recognized non-drug strategy for managing migraines, involving the identification and avoidance of trigger foods such as aged cheeses, processed meats, and alcohol. It is particularly useful for patients with identifiable dietary triggers and is often used in conjunction with other lifestyle modifications. The integration of dietary changes into treatment plans reflects a personalized approach to migraine management, especially for those who prefer to avoid medication [Data: Entities (242), Relationships (378, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Dietary regulation as a trigger-specific migraine intervention\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Mindfulness-based therapy is a non-drug treatment modality that has demonstrated efficacy in reducing migraine frequency and improving coping mechanisms. It is included in the non-drug treatment category and is particularly beneficial for patients with stress-related or chronic migraines. By promoting awareness and emotional regulation, mindfulness helps reduce the impact of psychological triggers and enhances overall resilience to migraine episodes [Data: Entities (244), Relationships (381, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Mindfulness therapy as a psychological intervention for migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Biofeedback therapy is a behavioral intervention used in both acute and preventive migraine treatment. It involves training patients to control physiological responses such as muscle tension and heart rate, which can contribute to migraine onset. This method is particularly effective for patients with tension-type or stress-induced migraines and is often used in combination with other non-drug approaches. Its inclusion in the community underscores the role of self-regulation in migraine management [Data: Entities (237), Relationships (371, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Biofeedback as a self-regulation-based migraine treatment\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Gepants are a class of medications used for both acute and preventive treatment of migraines. They function by targeting the calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) pathway, a key mediator in migraine pathophysiology. Gepants offer a non-triptan alternative for patients who cannot tolerate triptans or have contraindications. Their inclusion in the community highlights the evolution of pharmacological options beyond traditional triptans, particularly in the context of neurostimulation and other non-drug therapies [Data: Entities (238), Relationships (370, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Gepants as a modern pharmacological alternative in migraine treatment\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The interplay between pharmacological and non-pharmacological treatments is central to the community’s structure. Treatments like frovatriptan, neurostimulation, and gepants are often used in parallel or sequentially, depending on patient response and preference. This integrative approach reflects a shift toward personalized, multimodal migraine care. The high degree of relationships between these entities suggests a well-connected network of treatment options, supporting a flexible and adaptive clinical strategy [Data: Entities (151, 236, 238), Relationships (368, 370, 371, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Integrated treatment strategies combining drug and non-drug approaches\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the clinical significance of migraine and the diverse, well-integrated treatment options available, which influence patient outcomes and healthcare delivery.\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community centers on migraine management, integrating pharmacological treatme